section=\"\"\"## The proposed standard library: its layering criteria and its primitives

**Extraction decision.** Start with the repeated backward-liveness equations in the allocator and coalescer. They are two real clients within one compiler. Their different downstream needs (interval construction and copy interference) put a useful limit on the API. The graph, use/def projection and traversal scope suffice. A shared RV32 instruction class, register allocator or optimization policy would add coupling. The CPU probe exposes an important wrinkle: solving only from entry changes the fact returned for an unreachable predecessor. Preserve each caller’s coverage contract. "'`src/typed/backend/regalloc.ts:85`, `src/typed/backend/regalloc.ts:121`, `src/typed/backend/coalesce.ts:27`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:296`, `scripts/08-library-boundaries.mjs:20`.

### Layering criteria

| Owner | Admission test | Responsibility and limit |
|---|---|---|
| Generic core | Removing this facility would break the meaning of composition, identity or acceptance even for a domain with no CFG, numeric values or hardware backend. | Versioned artifact/port references, projection identity, candidate isolation, dependency records, validation outcomes and publication, strategy control, and the shape of a correspondence or premise. An execution can say which validator ran and what it concluded without knowing floating-point arithmetic. The existing manager and strategy already expose suitable integration points. `src/framework/contracts/analysis-manager.ts:518`, `src/framework/contracts/analysis-manager.ts:1577`, `src/framework/strategy/interpreter.ts:134`. |
| Standard library | There is a named algorithm or law, a small input vocabulary, at least two credible clients and a way to check the output independently. The clients can be different phases or independent cells. | Optional algorithms on plain graph, value, relation, observation or event views. A library result can be computed without a compiler session; register it as a versioned analysis, validator, metric or candidate builder when revision tracking matters. A concrete lattice, proof procedure or estimator has an owner here. A proposed second client is labelled below. |
| Domain | Correctness or utility depends on language evaluation, memory layout, ABI, target scheduling, host effects, or what the experiment is trying to measure. | Supply the views, operator interpretation, initial facts, alias/trap rules, observations, cost objectives, and edit/emission callbacks. For example, Tix'"'s reduction order and CUDA launch barriers constrain scheduling, while Typed chooses how a local is initialized and what register classes can coalesce. "'`src/tensor/evaluate.ts:81`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:246`, `src/typed/ssa.ts:6`, `src/typed/backend/coalesce.ts:29`. |

**Dependency direction.** Domains instantiate library algorithms. Optional registrations connect library values to core ports. Core has no dependency on the catalogue. A standalone algorithm receives a projected value or graph and returns an immutable result or refusal. The registration binds that result to exact revisions, parameters, profile and algorithm version. A domain edit still goes through candidate validation. Reusing the calculation across projects does not itself warrant reusing a cached answer across revisions.

**Promotion test.** Name both clients, the common law, the code to retire and one input on which an incautious extraction changes meaning. An algorithm that requires a switch on language or target should expose that semantic operation as an input, or stay with its existing owner. Share an implementation only after comparing results on both clients and measuring any projection cost. The proposed `stdlib` is a logical ownership boundary; the first extraction can occur in place. The signatures below are proposals. Their replacement sites are source observations. Probe results and untested extensions are identified explicitly.

### Graphs and dataflow

1. **Dominance, dominance frontiers and natural loops.** `dominance(cfg, entry) → {reachable, idom, frontier}`; `naturalLoops(cfg, dom) → {header, latches, body, exits, externalPredecessors}[]`. Dominance means every entry path passes through the dominator. For a natural loop, merge the backwards predecessor closures of all dominance backedges with the same header. Preserve the reachable-node set and distinguish irreducible cycles from natural loops. Replace the graph walks in Typed dominance, SSA frontier construction and loop discovery. Keep the Typed computation of assigned locals and the test for a usable preheader in the client. SSA consumes frontiers; LICM consumes the loop and its domain facts. Register both as revision-scoped queries. The merged two-latch fixture and the virtual-entry fixture are acceptance tests. `src/typed/analyses.ts:140`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:215`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:239`, `src/typed/ssa.ts:75`, `tests/typed/attribute.test.mjs:58`, `tests/typed/ssa.test.mjs:63`. [LLVM loop terminology](https://llvm.org/docs/LoopTerminology.html)

2. **Monotone dataflow, lattices and bit-vector analyses.** `solve(graph, scope, direction, lattice, boundary, transfer, edgeRefine?, widen?) → in/out`; `liveness(useDef, observableExits)` and `reachingDefs(genKill, entryDefs)` instantiate it. In backward liveness, OUT is the union of successor IN sets and IN = USE ∪ (OUT ∖ DEF). Supply powerset, flat constants, intervals and pointwise maps. Encodings may use bitsets while preserving the same lattice. Reuse the existing solver and lattice definitions; replace repeated machine backward sweeps and the common equations in Typed liveness/reaching definitions. Instruction uses, physical register effects, integer overflow and widening thresholds stay explicit. Coalescing consumes live-after; allocation builds intervals; dead-code elimination consumes liveness plus effects. The probe found {64,65} vs ∅ for the unreachable block. An all-blocks least fixed point and an entry-reachable result must have different scope keys. `src/framework/dataflow/lattices.ts:22`, `src/framework/dataflow/lattices.ts:45`, `src/framework/dataflow/lattices.ts:66`, `src/framework/dataflow/lattices.ts:93`, `src/framework/dataflow/solver.ts:90`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:352`, `src/typed/backend/regalloc.ts:97`, `src/typed/backend/coalesce.ts:32`, `scripts/08-library-boundaries.mjs:40`. [Cousot on abstract interpretation](https://www.di.ens.fr/~cousot/AI/)

3. **SSA construction as a read-only view.** `buildSSA(cfg, dom, orderedDefsUses, entryDefinitions) → {defOfUse, phiInputs, originOfDef}`. Place φ functions at iterated dominance frontiers and rename along the dominator tree. Entry definitions and unreachable blocks are explicit; clients with parallel CFG edges need predecessor-edge identity where values differ. Extract placement and renaming from Typed. Retain local names, initial zero values, parameter IDs and statement traversal in its projection. SCCP consumes the def-use graph; the reaching-definitions checker independently checks the constructed view. Pruned SSA using liveness is a later selectable policy. The entry-loop fixture matters because the virtual entry supplies a real incoming value. `src/typed/ssa.ts:60`, `src/typed/ssa.ts:95`, `src/typed/ssa.ts:219`, `src/typed/ssa.ts:325`, `src/typed/sccp.ts:47`, `tests/typed/ssa.test.mjs:63`. [Cytron et al. on SSA construction](https://research.ibm.com/publications/efficiently-computing-static-single-assignment-form-and-the-control-dependence-graph)

4. **Sparse conditional constant propagation (SCCP).** `sccp(ssa, entryValues, fold, feasibleSuccessors) → {abstractValues, executableEdges, justifications}`. Compute the coupled fixed point of value information and executable edges; join φ operands only from feasible predecessors. Extract the solver and typed-constant lattice coordination from Typed. Keep operation evaluation, trap behavior and branch conventions local. A value simplifier can consume constant facts and a CFG simplifier can consume infeasible edges. Each applies through a separate checked candidate. Unknown operations conservatively return overdefined. The existing decided-branch fixture is the first differential check; using this primitive to simplify a residual VM is conjectural. `src/typed/sccp.ts:23`, `src/typed/sccp.ts:47`, `src/typed/sccp.ts:141`, `src/typed/passes.ts:352`, `tests/typed/ssa.test.mjs:99`. [Wegman and Zadeck on conditional propagation](https://research.ibm.com/publications/constant-propagation-with-conditional-branches)

5. **Local value numbering.** `number(op, type, literalBits, operandNumbers, stateVersion) → congruenceClass`; `available(class, point) → dominatingRepresentative?`. This is congruence under deterministic operators with equal operands and relevant state. Replace the repeated tree-key logic in local value numbering and loop-invariant expression sharing. Keep the placement decision, scope, purity, traps and alias invalidation with each pass. A proof of equal values does not permit moving an expression that may trap onto a new path. Use SSA names or kill old representatives on assignment; retain a structural equality check for hash collisions. Return representative sites as edit premises. Check a repeated pure expression and the same expression across a local redefinition. `src/typed/passes.ts:480`, `src/typed/passes.ts:525`, `src/typed/passes.ts:541`, `src/typed/passes.ts:561`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:40`. [Cornell on local value numbering](https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs6120/2023fa/lesson/3/)

6. **Transitive dependence closure and slicing.** `closure(roots, successors, direction) → members`; `sliceKey(members, revisionOfMember, externalInputs) → key`. Forward reachability answers which callees must be present for execution; reverse reachability answers which callers a mutation may affect. Extract repeated walks in function-part reuse and mutation testing. Keep call-target resolution, missing callees, frame-layout dependencies and copying of a subprogram in Typed. The core query records the resulting dependencies. A recursive call cycle must terminate and a changed external input must invalidate the result even when membership is unchanged. `src/typed/parts.ts:123`, `src/typed/parts.ts:139`, `src/typed/parts.ts:241`, `src/typed/mutate.ts:416`, `src/typed/mutate.ts:442`, `src/typed/mutate.ts:649`. [Adapton on demand-driven dependencies](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~mwh/papers/hammer13adapton.html)

### Scheduling, specialization and numerical semantics

7. **Affine expressions, access relations and dependence tests.** `normalize(expr)`, `substitute(map, bindings)` and `violations(iterationDomain, accesses, requiredOrder, candidateSchedule) → empty | counterexample(pair) | unknown`. Canonical integer affine forms support composition; integer-relation emptiness checks whether a required dependence is reversed. Extract Tix’s coefficient arithmetic and substitution first. Replace recognized axis-prefix tests and finite reduction-order checks only for a proven subset. Shape bounds, alias hypotheses, ordered reductions, synchronization and target index width are inputs. Inline uses substitution; fusion and tiling use the same access maps; a schedule validator returns a scoped judgment to the core. Begin with constant tile factors and the extent-17 counterexample, then measure the coverage gain of a general solver. `src/tensor/model.ts:8`, `src/tensor/model.ts:19`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:12`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:97`, **T6-D** `src/tensor/schedule.ts:298`, `scripts/05-reduction-order.mjs:15`. [isl manual](https://libisl.sourceforge.io/user.html)

8. **Binding-time analysis.** `divide(defUse, staticInputs, operationDivision, memoryAssumptions) → {staticSites, dynamicSites, reasons}`. Offline partial evaluation computes a safe division for later specialization. Extract the dependency iteration in B1-F/G; domain transfer establishes which loads, traps and assignments are determined by the bindings. Return a conservative dynamic fact when a buffer can be mutated through an alias. Use the lattice solver for cyclic dependencies and key the query on the bound inputs as well as the function. The residualizer consumes the division; an inspection tool can report the first dynamic premise. The earlier B1-G probe demonstrates agreement on cyclic copies only. **B1-F** `src/typed/pe.ts:76`, **B1-G** `src/typed/bta.ts:65`, `src/typed/bta.ts:91`, **B1-A** `src/typed/specialize-memory.ts:154`, `scripts/02-binding-times.mjs:36`. [Jones, Gomard and Sestoft on partial evaluation](https://raspi.itu.dk/people/sestoft/pebook/toc.html)

9. **Polyvariant residualization.** `residualize(entry, staticEnv, step, liveProjection, generalize, budget) → residualGraph + assumptions | refusal`. Memoize a residual handle before exploring successors so repeated static states close loops. Extract B1-B'"'s and B1-F's version tables and queues. Use BTA or online interpretation, project keys to live static values, and return origin edges for emitted operations. Keep VM instruction semantics, guard construction and residual entry installation in the domain builder. State-count and output-size budgets are independent. On exhaustion either generalize under a justified policy or discard the private candidate. Compare guarded and unguarded VM variants; shape-specialized launch code remains a possible second domain. **B1-B** "'`src/typed/residualize.ts:61`, **B1-F** `src/typed/pe.ts:202`, `src/typed/pe.ts:224`, **B1-G** `src/typed/specialize-buffer.ts:288`.

10. **Binary32 arithmetic and observations by semantic profile.** `eval32(profile, op, operandWords) → resultWord | unsupported`; `equal32(observation, a, b)`. Separate representation, rounding and operation semantics from the observation relation. Extract word conversion, NaN classification and the shared rounded arithmetic subset from Typed and Tix; retain target rules for min/max, conversions, NaN payloads, contraction and transcendental functions as explicit bindings. The Typed folder and Tix reference evaluator can share compatible operations; an independent oracle should still test the implementation. On the same NaN and 1, Typed fmax returned 1 while Tix max produced NaN. Also test negative zero and finite rounding boundaries. “Exact” identifies a stated profile and observation; it cannot select them. `src/typed/f32.ts:27`, `src/typed/f32.ts:40`, `src/typed/f32.ts:57`, `src/tensor/evaluate.ts:19`, `src/tensor/evaluate.ts:79`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:105`, `scripts/08-library-boundaries.mjs:12`. [Berkeley SoftFloat interface](https://www.jhauser.us/arithmetic/SoftFloat-3/doc/SoftFloat.html)

### Validation, explanation and measurement

11. **Rule verification and contextual replay.** `verify(schema, semantics, assumptions) → certificate | boundedEvidence | refutation`; `replay(edit, focus, binding, premises) → explainedFootprint`. Equational rewriting needs a valid schema, a valid instance and a compatible context. Extract verifier dispatch and replay from Typed’s modular-linear and bitwise rule checks and the A0 reconstructions. Algebra-specific procedures and domain premises remain explicit. A validator checks the reconstructed candidate and any residual changes before publishing a judgment. A proven i32 identity and an f32 law can use the same protocol while depending on different semantics. Refuse a changed sibling even if the focused rewrite is valid. `src/typed/rules/verify.ts:9`, `src/typed/rules/verify.ts:179`, `src/typed/rules/compile.ts:124`, **A0-C** `tools/edit-census.mjs:21`, `scripts/06-rewrite-scope.mjs:13`. [Lopes et al. on verified peephole optimizations](https://web.ist.utl.pt/nuno.lopes/pubs/alive-pldi15.pdf)

12. **Case generation and coverage.** `generate(recipe, constraints, seed, budget) → cases + strata`; `coverage(attempted, comparable, obligations) → covered + gaps`. Property-based testing uses generators for the valid input domain. Share finite products, dependent choices, boundary values, seeded sampling and coverage bookkeeping. Replace the common recipe mechanics in Typed strong cases and Tix shape/stream/arm cases. Keep memory validity, correlated dimensions, NaN policy and the counterexample oracle in each domain. The differential validator records the recipe and coverage. Mutation testing can evaluate a recipe by which known changes it detects; invalid, equivalent and undecided mutants need distinct accounting. Check reproducibility and regenerate cases when the input grammar changes. `src/typed/cases-strong.ts:233`, `src/typed/cases-strong.ts:329`, `src/typed/mutate.ts:535`, `src/tensor/contract.ts:24`, `src/tensor/contract.ts:130`, `src/tensor/cuda-contract.ts:146`, `tests/typed/mutate.test.mjs:140`. [Claessen and Hughes on QuickCheck](https://research.chalmers.se/en/publication/155860)

